The '''History of Guajicutea (2020–present)''' covers the covid-era recession, the rebound in tourism, and the return of more openly contested politics after the long Partido Bukaí recovery years. The period began with a public-health crisis, a sharp economic contraction, and a wave of protests over the government's pandemic response. It later shifted into a period of recovery and social tension shaped by housing costs, retirement politics, and renewed coalition bargaining.

== The pandemic and recession ==

[[Rafael Meraí]] won the 2020 election, but the covid pandemic reached Guajicutea in March and rapidly changed the terms of government. At first, the administration resisted a full lockdown. The strategy left the island exposed, and deaths rose sharply. After the first wave, the government reversed course and imposed a very harsh lockdown, which in turn triggered protests over lost income, police enforcement, and the economic cost of confinement.

The result was a deep recession in 2021. Tourism collapsed, public events disappeared, and transport activity fell sharply. The island's service economy, which depended heavily on visitors and movement, was hit harder than in earlier downturns because so much of the economy had become tied to hospitality, short-term travel, and city-based retail. The recession was broad enough to affect housing, government revenues, and small business credit.

== Recovery and the 2022 coalition ==

By 2022, the economy had begun to recover. Tourism rebounded quickly as travel resumed, and Guajicutea again became a popular destination for visitors from nearby middle-class societies. The larger regional economies of [[Lacashe]], [[Asikyira]], and [[Balboa]] helped drive the rebound. Their expanding middle classes returned to Guajicutea for short trips, beach holidays, shopping, and family travel.

Politically, the 2022 election brought a major shift in the Assembly. A coalition of the [[Partido Kadarismo]], [[Partido Verde]], and [[Partido Popular Guaji]] won enough support to form a governing majority. The coalition was a reaction against the economic damage of the pandemic years and the belief that liberal reform alone had not prevented the crisis. The coalition pushed a more interventionist economic line and greater social protection, while [[Rafael Meraí]] remained president until 2024.

The recovery years also saw the state repay the [[Adonian Community]] loan earlier than expected, thanks to the tourism rebound and stronger revenue flows. That gave the government more room to rework policy without carrying the debt burden of the 2000s.

== Housing and retirement politics ==

Even as the economy recovered, the country faced a major housing crisis. Guajicutea had already become a popular retirement destination for people from [[Sarta]] during the mid-2010s, and foreign Sartan buyers continued to purchase homes in major cities. The result was a sharp rise in housing prices, especially in the capital and the biggest coastal cities, where local wages lagged behind property values.

The 2022–2024 coalition responded by banning the purchase of homes by foreign buyers in the most affected urban areas and creating a fund for retirees, since Guajicutea had never developed a national pension system. The policy did not solve the underlying shortage, but it signaled a new willingness to treat housing as a public issue rather than just a market one.

In 2023, older residents staged a major protest demanding a national pension scheme. The demonstration exposed how quickly the country was aging and how weak the existing retirement structure had become. The issue became one of the main social debates of the decade.

== Labour politics and social policy ==

The pandemic and the inflationary recovery also revived labour politics. Workers in tourism, retail, logistics, and public services pushed harder for wages that kept up with living costs. The government did not return to the confrontational anti-union politics of the Mera era, but it still had to balance business interests, tourism recovery, and labour demands.

The social coalition that had emerged after 2020 also kept several reforms from the previous decade in place. The minimum wage continued to rise with inflation, and the broader rights framework of the 2010s remained intact. By this period, the political argument had shifted away from whether Guajicutea should have social protections and toward how far those protections should go and who should pay for them.

== The 2024 election ==

In 2024, the political balance shifted again. A new alliance of the [[Partido Popular Guaji]] and the [[Partido Bukaí]] won the presidency, bringing [[Sofía Yayaí]] to office and ending the coalition rule that had dominated the previous two years. The result reflected fatigue with crisis management, housing pressure, and the slow pace of pension reform, but it also reflected the continuing strength of tourism and service-sector voters who wanted a more pro-growth government.

The change did not reverse the social reforms of the early 2020s. Instead, it produced a more market-friendly coalition that still had to acknowledge the housing problem, the aging population, and the need to keep tourism resilient after the pandemic shock.

== Public mood and culture ==

The culture of the early 2020s was shaped by recovery, caution, and memory. The pandemic made public health and state capacity unavoidable topics. The lockdown protests, the housing crisis, and the pension protests all sharpened political feeling, but they also showed that Guajicutea's politics had become more organized and more issue-driven than in the chaotic Mera era.

Tourism returned to the center of public life after the pandemic shock, but it did so in a more self-conscious setting. Restaurants, beaches, and city centers recovered, yet they did so alongside arguments about housing access, retirement, and the rights of workers. The island remained outward-looking, but it was less naive about dependence on tourism than it had been before 2020.

== Economy and employment ==

The economy remained service-led. Tourism, transport, and retail rebounded first after the recession. Call centers, administrative outsourcing, and finance-adjacent services continued to expand, though more slowly than in the optimistic 2010s. Construction also returned as tourism projects resumed and port repairs were completed after the 2019 hurricane.

The overall pattern was one of partial recovery rather than a return to the old boom years. The state had more fiscal space than it did in 2020, but the pandemic, housing costs, and demographic aging meant that the economy had to rely on a more complex mix of tourism, services, regulation, and immigration than before.

